1|0@MensRights^1438576126#ctpl4gz:dangerousopinions$:&gt;As a Canadian, you don't get a say, and it's kind of insulting that you think you do. Oh I can't have an opinion on the policy of other countries? I wasn't aware. I guess I better start being more insular and only care about things that happen in Canada then. /s &gt;You people still bow and scrape before the queen, FFS. She can dissolve your government with the stroke of a pen. She can refuse to seat duly elected officials. The Queen is a symbolic figure. She has not dissolved government or refused to recognize elected officials and if she did, she would be ignored and written out of power with the stroke of a pen. The Queen is the head of commonwealth states as a nod to history, not for any practical function. This might be silly, but it's not in anyway a risk to anyone's freedom, independence or sovereignty, at least in Canada. &gt;You have nothing to teach us about freedom. You don't even have freedom of speech over there. You don't an Equal Protection clause to your foundational documents. It's true that hate speech is technically a crime in Canada but it's also true that the courts have not affirmed this law in the time that it's existed and on the few occasions cases have made it to the supreme court they've repeatedly struck down rulings from lower courts. The SCC hasn't said that the concept of the law itself is unconstitutional but they have yet to uphold any use of the law and have more and more narrowly defined what might constitute hate speech. So in practical terms, Canada has exactly the same freedom of speech the United States does and it is quite robust. As far as an equal protection clause, I have no idea what you're talking about and you're completely wrong. In fact in many ways equal protection is much more inclusive in Canada than the U.S. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Section_Fifteen_of_the_Canadian_Charter_of_Rights_and_Freedoms &gt;Americans understand that freedom is only secured by the barrel of a gun. That's probably because the United States secured independence with the barrel of a gun. That's not the only way it happens and by and large, revolutions leading to anything good are quite rare. Most change happens more gradually. If you look at the French Revolution, what followed was so awful it was referred to as "the terror". It took another 100 years to form a reasonably fair democracy. In the case of Canada, most of Europe and many of the British Commonwealth countries, democracy was born out of slow, mostly bloodless change. Canada arrived at the same place the United States did within 100 years and didn't have to sacrifice tens of thousands of lives to do it. Beyond that, your government regularly ignores the constitutional rights of its citizens. It's habitual at this point and often supported by the supreme court. And yet you have the gall to say that you're keeping them in line by the barrel of a gun? If that's the case you're doing a shit job. 
