0|1@MensRights^1438551288#ctp8mae:dangerousopinions$:Listen, this is not the discussion being had in this thread. You questioned a specific sentence in my comment and I explained why I said it and why it made sense. You don't have to agree. I very much disagree with your take on things for a variety of reasons, but again, gun control itself is not the point of this post, or my comment or this thread and I am not going to get into a debate with you about it. Edit:I'm going to address your points since this discussion has gone that direction. &gt;There's nothing that stops people from skirting the law period.There's nothing that stops people from skirting the law period. It can be mitigated a great deal. If laws were entirely useless and unenforceable, as a general rule we would just have anarchy, but we don't. Evidently, laws can be enforced and in the case of guns, regulations can greatly reduce the number of illegal weapons available to criminals. This has the effect of raising prices and making them harder for the average criminal to get their hands on. &gt;So if I lived in MA and wanted a gun but couldn't legally buy one, instead of driving up to NH, I'd buy one on the streets in a rougher part of Boston like Roxbury or Murderpan. Not if the regulation was effective. This is especially true in the U.S, which is unique in the world because most of the guns on the illegal market, were initially sold legally. This is not the case elsewhere where illegal guns have to be smuggled into the country. &gt;And in truth, it isn't like most of the people doing most of the killings are buying legal guns and carrying them legally, ect. No, of course not. But have you ever wondered where these guns come from in the first place? They're manufactured in the United States by legal entities, sold to legal gun owners by licensed dealers and then as a result of poor or non-existent regulations they make it onto the illegal market either through straw purchases or completely unregulated second hand sales. In most states you can sell a handgun on craigslist to practically anyone in a Walmart parking lot without any documentation and without breaking any laws. The ATF has done a fair bit of research and found that 90% of illegal handguns were sold in straw purchases. They've also found that a small percentage of licensed dealers "lose" a massive volume of guns (many or most of which end up being sold to criminals). What can be done about this? Under the current laws, practically nothing. The ATF is basically neutered when dealing with sketchy dealers and they can get away with bad or inaccurate inventory and missing sales documents without any recourse. The issues that create illegal weapons in the United States are by and large easily fixed with regulation and minimal enforcement. A lot of it is just clamping down on dealers (which has been repeatedly and successfully opposed by the NRA multiple times) and giving the ATF the power to keep computerized records of dealers sales so they can find out who's following the rules and who's selling guns to criminals and claiming them as lost. Another thing that is effective, at least in Canada, is limiting impulse gun sales to irresponsible people by requiring a weekend course or just waiting for paperwork to make its way through the system. In addition to this, in Canada you must produce your ownership license to purchase ammunition. This is not a major inconvenience for legal owners and it creates another hurdle for criminals. It seems though, that you hear "gun control" and you think that that must mean "gun bans". That's almost never the case, at least as far as both sides of the debate are concerned in the U.S. 
